# Ordinary initialization report and diagnostic connection

This held Source proposal connects the accepted postlude, full predefined
registry, genuine sizing inputs and real severe/backend callpoints to both
ordinary ep_run heat-balance paths. No canonical source, Git, target, formatter,
Cargo command, Native engine, numerical result or binary was changed or read.

The caller owns CtfInitializationReports: full registry/table allocations, the
real Scan cache and reached postlude progress. CtfInitializationContext borrows
that owner, the actual EIO writer and an explicit SourceErrorBackend. Registry
creation consumes the genuine retained SimulationControl flags. No column IDs
or sizing flags are supplied by a fixture or guessed from downstream behavior.

The cache invokes loading and retry calculations once, dispatching each reached
severe/continue/fatal through the same backend at its original callpoint. Original
loader names come from the compiler-retained names and are checked against their
typed identity. Loading name lookup is deferred to the actual formatting call,
so an unavailable later name cannot erase an earlier severe output. Unused rows
do not acquire a new name prerequisite. Selected-chain/public scope stops return
immediately before any later construction callbacks or stores.

After the true ordered producer loop, the actual final flags reach the accepted
layers/Scan/conditional-EIO/final-fatal postlude. That call precedes the existing
surface lifecycle allocation. Legacy coefficient, material and hash calculations
retain their existing expressions. Explicit diagnostic APIs remain available
with their existing signatures and do not silently gain output requirements.

Both production weather runtime siblings borrow this context at the real state
initialization call. Default ep_run A/B dispatch uses these siblings. ep_run owns
the registry/table/Scan/counters and output buffers outside the runtime Result,
then persists exact eplusrs.ctf.err, eplusrs.eio and eplusrs.ctf.log bytes before
handling success or failure. Both final error-summary paths prepend the exact
stored Source prefix to their normal structured diagnostics. Fatal output is
therefore not discarded by Result-to-String conversion or summary replacement.

The ordinary Rust caller explicitly creates absent SQLite, callback and optional
output-unit owners because RunConfig has none. It retains a real buffered console
writer separately from CLI JSON stdout. Header strings identify the actual Rust
package and typed input version. Native VerStringVar/IDDVerString, backend bytes,
callbacks and database parity are not claimed. The generic borrowed backend still
supports actual optional owners supplied by another caller. General tabular
rendering remains separate; no alternate predefined export format is invented.

Actual io::Error and typed fatal errors remain inside RuntimeError and its Error
source chain. Its established PartialEq API compares failure variants and actual
OS error kind/code/text; it does not replace errors with text-only markers.

Four unexecuted tests cover a genuine cache-to-registry/table/lifecycle connection,
loading error followed by retained layers and final fatal, a missing reached name
that preserves the earlier thick-material severe, and ep_run fatal-prefix merging.
They reuse the existing cube input fixture; no physics expectation is retuned.

Before sources are explicitly actual historical archives or accepted prospective
postlude/severe bodies. Root subsequently installed their cumulative24 batch;
that actual record is documentary authority, not a claim that these historical
before bytes still equal live formatted parents. A minimal actual-parent rebase
and genuine formatter/current-source bindings remain required before application.
The three new canonical destinations were absent at preparation and remain future
sources. Root and an independent FULL Source peer must accept this hold first.
No whole Init, report/backend byte, production numerical or full-card PASS is
claimed. The author's earlier backend/preprocessor/postlude authorship is disclosed.
